# Two properties that a VENDOR process reads or writes but that do NOT live in
# the vendor namespace, so they cannot be labelled from sepolicy/vendor:
#
#   check_prop_prefix --strict: 2 violations found
#     ro.sys.battery.FirstUsageDate
#     ro.rkp.csr_uploaded
#   vendor's and odm's property_contexts MUST use ONLY vendor-prefixed
#   properties. This is enforced by VTS.
#
# Unlabelled they fall on default_prop, and that is a dead end in BOTH
# directions -- default_prop is system_internal_prop (public/property.te:272), so
#   * no vendor domain may ever be granted the read
#     (private/property.te: neverallow { domain -coredomain }
#      { system_property_type ... -system_public_property_type }:file
#      no_rw_file_perms), and
#   * nothing but init may set it
#     (private/property.te:796 neverallow { domain -init }
#      default_prop:property_service set).
#
# WHY exported_default_prop AND NOT A NEW TYPE. The first attempt declared
# Motorola's own moto_battinfo_prop / rkp_config_prop here as public system_ext
# types, the way stock does. That fails this tree's build in two separate ways:
#
#   treble_sepolicy_tests_{30.0,31.0,32.0,33.0,34.0,202404}:
#     The following public types were found added to the policy without an entry
#     into the compatibility mapping file(s) ... moto_battinfo_prop rkp_config_prop
#   sepolicy_neverallows:
#     neverallow ... violated by allow vendor_init rkp_config_prop:property_service
#     { set }   (system_restricted_property_type may not be set by a non-coredomain)
#
# The first would require editing six compat .cil files inside system/sepolicy
# for every future API level; the second would force the type public anyway.
#
# exported_default_prop is AOSP's own bucket for exactly this case -- a
# system-namespace property that vendor code touches. It is
# system_vendor_config_prop (public/property.te:156), which expands to
# system_public_prop + set_prop(vendor_init, ...) + a neverallow limiting writes
# to init/vendor_init, and private/domain.te:522 already gives EVERY domain the
# read. So these two lines need no accompanying allow rule anywhere, add no new
# type, and need no compat mapping.
#
# ro.sys.battery.FirstUsageDate -- polled by /vendor/bin/batt_health roughly
# once a second. 14537 of the 15603 denials in a 4.9 h capture, 93% of the whole
# log. Stock labels it moto_battinfo_prop in system_ext_property_contexts:422
# and grants batt_health the read; exported_default_prop is the same access with
# no new type.
ro.sys.battery.FirstUsageDate   u:object_r:exported_default_prop:s0

# ro.rkp.csr_uploaded -- set by /vendor/bin/init.mmi.boot.sh (running as
# vendor_init) after the remote key-provisioning CSR upload. Two denials per
# boot. Stock: system_ext_property_contexts:406 (rkp_config_prop).
# vendor_init already holds set on exported_default_prop
# (private/vendor_init.te:290).
ro.rkp.csr_uploaded             u:object_r:exported_default_prop:s0

# ro.mot.build.customerid -- read by /vendor/bin/thermal-engine-v2,
# /vendor/bin/rmt_storage and vendor/lib64/libqcrilNr.so (all vendor-side; no
# coredomain reader in this build). Unlabelled it lands on default_prop, which
# is system_internal_prop and can never be read by a vendor domain; it is part
# of vendor_thermal-engine's residual `{ read } default_prop` denials.
#
# ro.mot. is not a vendor prefix, so this cannot go in
# ../../vendor/property_contexts -- same check_prop_prefix constraint as the two
# entries above.
#
# THIS ONE USES STOCK'S EXACT TYPE rather than exported_default_prop:
# system_ext_property_contexts:268 says `build_prop`, which is
# system_restricted_prop (public/property.te:66) and therefore explicitly exempt
# from the non-coredomain read neverallow -- several vendor domains in this tree
# already hold get_prop(..., build_prop). The reader grant for thermal-engine is
# in ../../vendor/vendor_thermal-engine.te.
ro.mot.build.customerid         u:object_r:build_prop:s0

# sys.arcfox.powershare -- the reverse-wireless-charging (power share) toggle.
# Set by the ArcfoxPowerShare app (system_app), read by vendor_init to trigger the
# write in init.arcfox-powershare.rc.
#
# exported_system_prop is the UNIQUE type that works here, not a stylistic choice:
#   - init's action_parser IsActionableProperty() REJECTS a vendor-rc property
#     trigger at parse time unless vendor_init can read the name, and this device
#     has ro.actionable_compatible_property.enabled=true.
#   - a bare sys.* is system_prop, which vendor_init cannot read.
#   - a vendor.* name can never be set by a coredomain (property.te neverallow).
#   - exported_system_prop is system_public_prop; private/domain.te already gives
#     vendor_init the read and private/system_app.te the set, and the
#     compatible_property_only neverallow exempts coredomain/appdomain.
# So: no new type, no new allow rule. If this line is missing the toggle is simply
# inert (init logs "unexported property trigger found") -- no boot risk.
sys.arcfox.powershare           u:object_r:exported_system_prop:s0
